# 07-AddVisualFeedback

Add visual feedback to help users understand which RGB sphere is selected
and make the light sources appear luminous.

## Overview

In this section, you will add two visual elements to the RGB spheres:

- A soft radiating glow around each sphere.
- A white selection rim around the currently selected sphere.

These elements are visual only. They do not receive input or participate
in collision detection, so they do not interfere with sphere interaction.

### Add a Radiating Glow

Create a radial gradient texture and apply it to a billboarded plane.

@Code(name: "RGBSphere.swift", file: "07-VisualFeedback.swift")

The radial gradient is strongest at the center and gradually fades toward
the edges. The gradient uses the sphere's RGB color so each sphere produces
a matching colored glow.

The glow is attached as a child of the RGB sphere and uses a
`BillboardComponent` so that it always faces the viewer.

Instead of using another transparent sphere for the glow, the experience
uses a flat plane with a radial texture. This avoids making the RGB spheres
look like transparent balloons while still creating a soft luminous effect.

### Keep the Glow Non-Interactive

The glow is only a visual element.

Remove its `CollisionComponent`, `InputTargetComponent`, and
`ManipulationComponent` so that users interact with the RGB sphere itself
rather than the glow.

This keeps the visual layer separate from the interaction layer.

### Create a Selection Rim

Create a thin white ring around the currently selected sphere.

@Code(name: "SphericalSelectionRim.swift", file: "07-VisualFeedback.swift")

The selection rim is created as a custom annular mesh rather than a torus.
This keeps the ring visually simple and prevents unwanted geometry from
appearing through the transparent sphere.

A `BillboardComponent` keeps the rim facing the user.

### Show the Selected Sphere

When a sphere becomes selected, add the selection rim as a child of the
sphere.

@Code(name: "SphereInteractionManager.swift", file: "07-VisualFeedback.swift")

Before selecting another sphere, remove the existing selection rim.

This ensures that only one sphere is visually selected at a time.

### Keep the Selection Rim Non-Interactive

The selection rim should not interfere with manipulation.

Remove its `CollisionComponent`, `InputTargetComponent`, and
`ManipulationComponent`.

The user can therefore continue interacting with the underlying RGB sphere
while the rim provides visual feedback about the current selection.

## Result

The RGB spheres now have a soft colored glow and a clear selection
indicator.

The glow communicates that the spheres represent sources of light, while
the selection rim helps the user understand which sphere is currently being
manipulated.
